.th PSC I 1976
.sh NAME
psc \*- Pascal-S compiler/interpreter
.sh SYNOPSIS
.bd psc
[-l] [-tn] file
.sh DESCRIPTION
.bd psc
compiles the Pascal-S (a subset of Pascal (see below)) program
in "file" and if there are no errors detected, executes
the program.
The name "file" must end in ".p" to indicate it is a Pascal
source program. The following flags may be used:
.s1
.lp +9 8
-\fBl\fR	produces a full program listing on standard output.
Default is to list only lines that are in error along with the error
messages.
.s1
.lp +9 8
-\fBt\fIn\fR	\fIn\fR specifies an integer multiple of 1000
which is the limit on the number of program steps to be executed.
Default is 10000 program steps (i.e. -\fBt\fR10).
Specifying -\fBt\fR0
may be used to inhibit execution.
.i0
.dt
.s3
The input (output) for the executed program comes from (goes to)
standard input (output). For example:
.s1
	psc x.p <y
.s1
will run program "x.p" with input data from file "y".
.s3
Pascal-S includes only two kinds of data structures:
the array and the record (without variants).
Omitted are the set and the file structures.
The exceptions are the two standard text files input and output
which are declared implicitly (but must be listed in the program heading).
A very essential omission is pointer types and thereby
all dynamic structures.
Of course, all packing options (packed records, packed arrays)
are omitted.
.s3
The choice of data types and structures essentially determines the complexity
of the processing system.
Statement and control structures contribute but little to it.
Hence, Pascal-S includes most of Pascal's statement structures
(compound, conditional, selective, and repetitive statements).
The only omissions are the
.bd with
and the
.bd goto
statements.
The latter was omitted very deliberately because of the principal
use of Pascal-S in the teaching of systematic design of well-structured
programs.
Procedures and functions are included in their full
generality. The only exception is that procedures and functions cannot 
be used as parameters.
.sh "SEE ALSO"
N. Wirth, "Pascal-S: A Subset and its Implementation",
Institut fur Inormatik, ETH, Zurich (June 1975).
Unix Pascal User Manual, Report Appendix and PXP User Manual.
pc(I), pcx(I), pxp(I), pcc(I), pxref(I).
.sh DIAGNOSTICS
The compile time error messages tend to be rather cryptic (especially
for the novice. One fact that may be useful
is that the actual error in your program occured
at or BEFORE the error message.
The actual error in your program may be 
some distance back from the error message.
Commonly an error message will appear at the first token of
a line when the actual error was at the end of the previous
line. For example, if you leave a semicolon off the end of a line
when there is another statement following on the next line,
an error message will be printed out for that next line.
.s3
The following explanations of the error codes may help a little:
.s1
.lp +4 4
0.\ \ The designated identifier has not been declared.
.lp +4 4
1.\ \ The indicated identifier is declared more than once in the same scope.
.lp +4 4
2.\ \ An identifier is expected.
.lp +4 4
3.\ \ Every program must begin with the symbol
.bd program.
.lp +4 4
4.\ \ A closing parenthesis is expected.
.lp +4 4
5.\ \ A colon is expected. In declarations, the colon
is followed by a type.
.lp +4 4
6.\ \ At this point, the indicated symbol is incorrectly used.
The compiler skips this and possibly several following symbols.
.lp +4 4
7.\ \ In a formal parameter list each section must begin with an
identifier or the symbol
.bd var,
depending whether the parameter is a value or a variable parameter.
.lp +4 4
8.\ \ The symbol 
.bd of 
is expected.
.lp +4 4
9.\ \ An opening parenthesis is expected.
.lp +4 4
10.\ A type definition must begin with an identifier, the symbol
.bd array
or the symbol
.bd record.
.lp +4 4
11.\ An opening bracket is expected ([).
.lp +4 4
12.\ A closing bracket is expected (]).
.lp +4 4
13.\ The symbol .. is expected (no blank between the dots).
.lp +4 4
14.\ A semicolon is expected (;).
.lp +4 4
15.\ The result of a function must be of type integer, real,
Boolean, or char.
.lp +4 4
16.\ An equal sign is expected. The symbol := is used in assignment
statements only, not in declarations.
.lp +4 4
17.\ The expression following the symbol
.bd if,
.bd while,
or
.bd until
must be of type Boolean.
.lp +4 4
18.\ The control variable following the symbol
.bd for
must be of type integer, char, or Boolean.
.lp +4 4
19.\ The expressions which specify the initial and final values
of the control variable in a for statement must be of the same type
as the control variable.
.lp +4 4
20.\ The parameter "output" must be included in the program
heading.
.lp +4 4
21.\ The indicated number is too large. The maximum number of
digits allowed is 11; the absolute value must not exceed
10e32.
.lp +4 4
22.\ A dot is expected at the end of the program.
Check corresponding 
.bd begin
and 
.bd end
symbols!
.lp +4 4
23.\ The expression following the symbol
.bd case
must be of type integer, char, or Boolean. (In the latter case,
an if statement is recommended.)
.lp +4 4
24.\ The designated character is not acceptable.
.lp +4 4
25.\ In a constant definition, the equal sign must be followed
by a constant. If an identifier is used, it must denote a constant.
.lp +4 4
26.\ The type of an index expression must be identical to the
index type specified in the array declaration.
.lp +4 4
27.\ In an array declaration, the lower bound must not exceed
the upper bound. They must both be in the permissible range of values.
Also, their types must be identical, either integers, Boolean values, or
characters. Real numbers are not acceptable.
.lp +4 4
28.\ Every indexed variable must be declared as an array.
.lp +4 4
29.\ A type identifier is expected here.
.lp +4 4
30.\ This type is not defined. (Recursive type definitions
are not allowed.)
.lp +4 4
31.\ Every variable with a field selector must be declared
as a record.
.lp +4 4
32.\ The operands of the operators 
.bd not,
.bd and,
and
.bd or
must be of type Boolean.
.lp +4 4
33.\ The specified type of this arithetic expression is illegal.
Note also that entire arrays cannot occur as operands
to arithmetic and logical operators.
.lp +4 4
34.\ Operands of 
.bd div
and
.bd mod
must be of type integer.
.lp +4 4
35.\ The types of the comparands are incompatible.
They must be identical, except if one comparand is of type integer and the
other of type real. Arrays must be compared element by element.
.lp +4 4
36.\ The types of the corresponding actual and formal
parameters must be identical. An exception is made if the formal
parameter is a value parameter of type real. Then the actual
parameter may also be of type integer.
.lp +4 4
37.\ A variable is expected.
.lp +4 4
38.\ A string must contain at least one character.
.lp +4 4
39.\ The number of actual parameters must be equal to the number 
of specified formal parameters.
.lp +4 4
40.\ The parameters of the procedure read must be of type
char, integer, or real.
.lp +4 4
41.\ The parameters of the procedure write must be of type
char, integer, real, or Boolean.
.lp +4 4
42.\ If a statement has the form write(x:m:n), then x must
be an expression of type real.
.lp +4 4
43.\ If a statement has the form write(x:n) or write(x:m:n),
then m and n must be expressions of type integer.
.lp +4 4
44.\ No type or procedure identifiers may occur as part of
an expression.
.lp +4 4
45.\ A statement cannot begin with a type or a function identifier.
An exception is the assignment of a result value to a function.
In this case, it must be part of the function body.
.lp +4 4
46.\ In an assignment x := y , the types of the variable x and 
the expression y must be identical. An exception is the case
when x is a real. Then, y may also be of type integer.
.lp +4 4
47.\ Every case label must be a constant of the same type
as the expression in the case clause.
.lp +4 4
48.\ The indicated argument of the standard function is of an 
illegal type.
.lp +4 4
49.\ The program requires too much storage.
.lp +4 4
50.\ A constant cannot begin with the indicated symbol.
.lp +4 4
51.\ The symbol := is expected. (No space between : and =)
.lp +4 4
52.\ The symbol
.bd then
is expected.
.lp +4 4
53.\ The symbol 
.bd until
is expected.
.lp +4 4
54.\ The symbol
.bd do
is expected.
.lp +4 4
55.\ The symbol
.bd to
(or
.bd downto)
is expected.
.lp +4 4
56.\ The symbol 
.bd begin
is expected.
.lp +4 4
57.\ The symbol 
.bd end
is expected.
.lp +4 4
58.\ A factor must begin with an identifier, a constant, the
symbol
.bd not,
or with a left parenthesis.
.i0
.dt
.sh AUTHOR
The original author of Pascal-S was N. Wirth (written in Pascal
for the Cyber).
This Unix version written in "C" was implemented as a thesis
project by John O'Neill in 1976.
.sh BUGS
.bd psc
suffers from 7 fatal diseases - all to do with running out of space
because a program has too many or too much of one of the following:
.nf
.s1
	1. identifiers
	2. procedures and functions
	3. real number constants
	4. arrays
	5. levels of nesting of procedures
	6. code for the program
	7. strings of characters in write statements
.s1
.fi
Real input can be a bit ragged at the limits.
